WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.190
Create a small local benchmark set from real work.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:06.790
Include common cases, difficult cases, costly errors, expected formats,

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:07.995
and privacy-safe examples.

00:00:07.995 --> 00:00:10.940
Write acceptance criteria before seeing model results.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.940
Keep the configuration reproducible: model, quantization, runtime, backend, context,

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.265
prompt template, generation settings, hardware, drivers, and date.

00:00:19.265 --> 00:00:22.000
Changing several variables at once makes results difficult to interpret.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.190
Run important prompts more than once.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.590
Record accuracy, completeness, refusals, hallucinations, formatting, latency,

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.235
prompt processing, generation speed, memory, temperature, failures, and recovery.

00:00:32.235 --> 00:00:33.990
Stability is a quality characteristic.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.260
Evaluate whether refusals are appropriate and whether the model invents facts, citations,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.000
code behavior, or security conclusions.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.990
A smooth answer can still be wrong.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:45.250
Use task-specific review or automated checks where they are valid.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.990
Compare total workflow value.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.620
A smaller model may finish more tasks correctly because it is fast, stable,

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.990
and easy to retry.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.965
A larger model may reduce review on difficult tasks enough to justify its cost.

00:00:55.965 --> 00:00:59.842
Keep an evaluation log and define promotion or rollback criteria.

00:00:59.842 --> 00:01:03.960
Re-test when models, quantizations, runtimes, drivers, prompts, or hardware change.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.930
Choose based on evidence from the actual machine, not memory of an old leaderboard.
